# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, normalisation depends on each clone's local core.autocrlf,
# which is a machine setting and is not cloned. A contributor whose git is set
# differently can commit CRLF and produce a diff that touches every line of a
# file for no reason. These rules live in the repository, so they apply to
# everyone: Linux, macOS, Windows and CI alike.
#
# This matters more here than in most repos: sync-tools.ps1 copies raw bytes
# from the FPV Tools working tree, so whatever endings are on disk at that
# moment land in this repository. Pinning the stored form keeps those syncs
# from showing up as whole-file diffs.
#
# text=auto  -> git decides whether a file is text, and always stores LF.
#               The working tree still follows core.autocrlf, so Windows
#               checkouts keep getting CRLF on disk as before.
* text=auto

# Scripts whose interpreter cares about the ending, regardless of platform.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Web files are served and diffed as LF everywhere.
*.html   text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.css    text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf

# CNAME is read by GitHub Pages and has no extension.
CNAME    text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch these. A single byte changed by newline conversion
# corrupts a font or an image.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.webp   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
